\chapter{The repository and its evidence}
\label{ch:2}
\index{The repository and its evidence}

Start in \OnxCodeTerm{on1x/} for the compiler. The language grammar is in \OnxCodeTerm{grammar/}, source checking and C generation are in \OnxCodeTerm{src/}, checked-HIR passes are in \OnxCodeTerm{hir/}, adapters are in \OnxCodeTerm{backends/}, and generated programs share support in \OnxCodeTerm{runtime/}. The library, native and script processors, and two SDKs have their own directories. At the MuLIB root, \OnxCodeTerm{include/}, \OnxCodeTerm{manual/}, \OnxCodeTerm{examples/}, and \OnxCodeTerm{tests/} provide the kernel headers, usage guides, programs, and checks.

\section{Trace a feature across layers}
For a value-producing loop, start with the source manual, find the parser and canonical lowering, then compare the C11 execution test with the tests that construct malformed HIR directly. The source test exercises something a user can actually write. A forged-HIR test exercises the independent verifier when inference has been bypassed. Both matter to compiler consumers.

The capability matrix distinguishes executable profiles from partial ones. Verification entries sometimes refer to older revisions or different optional dependencies. Copy the revision, build flags, and target when quoting their results. For MuLIB, start with the manifest entry and inspect its header and companion manual before inferring a callable C ABI or an On1x wrapper.

\section{Read one manifest entry sideways}
Find the entry named \OnxCodeTerm{container-core}. Read its implementation
field before looking for a library to link: the field says
\OnxCodeTerm{static-inline}. Its dependency list is empty. Follow its
header, manual and example fields; they point respectively to declarations
and definitions, a usage contract, and a C client. A client can compile a
call to an inline helper by including the header. Searching a shared
library's exported symbols for that helper would test the wrong
proposition. For an ABI module the manifest describes a different model:
one implementation translation unit supplies definitions for the clients.

There is a similar distinction in the verification record for QUIC and
HTTP/3 registration. Read the result from the bottom up. A C11 client
linked against the archive, and import inspection produced declaration
graphs. Did an On1x program therefore call those declarations? The record
explicitly says that direct safe and unsafe call admission remained denied.
The tests agree: finding a description of a function and being allowed
to generate a call to it are different outcomes.

As a reading exercise, write two captions for that result, one for a C
library user and one for an On1x user. The former can cite the linked
client on the measured target. The latter can cite import inspection on
that configuration. Attaching the word ``supported'' to the protocol name
alone would lose the most useful information in both captions. Likewise,
an increased catalog count is evidence of more entries to inspect; by
itself it predicts no increase in the number of callable functions.

